it's really an honor for me to be back  in Connecticut with you here today so  thank you for the invitation it's an  honor to be representing the president's  hurricane sandy task force I grew up in  mystic and willimantic and my father is  a proud graduate of the Yale class of  nineteen forty one so thank you for  having me here and thank you senator  Murphy for the invitation we're coming  together at an important moment for a  long time the scientific community has  told the world that the climate is  changing posing an incredible threat to  us all in recent years we've seen that  the predictions have become reality the  12 hottest years on record have been in  the last 15 it feels like the storms  that used to occur every 100 years seem  to come every few years or in the recent  case of Oklahoma City in within ten days  of each other I had a meeting with  Governor Chris Christie of New Jersey a  few weeks ago and he said I don't know  why they call it the hundred year storm  since I've been in office there have  been three of them and I'm not 300 years  old if nothing is done as we've  discussed already the the risk facing  the region in the country will only  continue to grow so that while we know  that we have to cut carbon pollution by  moving to clean energy sources and  cutting our waste we also know that we  need to act now to prepare ourselves for  the effects of the climate that we're  not going to be able to mitigate against  as everyone here in Connecticut realizes  and throughout the entire New England  region know all too well they're not  theoretical future threats these are the  impacts already being felt because of  climate change while working on disaster  recovery at HUD over the past 13 years  I've seen up close just how devastating  the weather-related disasters can be in  places like New Orleans and Tuscaloosa  and Cedar Rapids and New York New Jersey  Connecticut Rhode Island in Pennsylvania  Delaware hurricane Sandy's fury caused  so much destruction throughout the  region damaging or destroying well over  six hundred thousand homes causing  unprecedented loss of power in the  region  size and scope of the damage done by  Sandy was unlike anything many of us in  this region had ever seen before and it  was clear in Washington that  extraordinary support for the region was  going to be required that's why the  president created the hurricane sandy  task force chaired by secretary Donovan  of HUD to bring cabinet level focus on  the work it wasn't just going to be a  FEMA question of getting in and  providing some relief dollars but really  rallying the entire cabinet a divided  Congress ultimately passed a  supplemental appropriation bill of more  than 50 billion dollars thanks to your  delegations tireless work we're very  grateful that we had the opportunity to  work with you so thanks again senator  once the task force was established we  quickly got to work our goals were to  cut red tape to get the assistance where  it was needed and effectively and  ultimately to outline principles to  guide the investment of federal dollars  since we knew that there would be tens  of billions of dollars in supplemental  flow simple dental assistants flowing  through region we wanted to ensure that  the building the rebuilding would be  done right while certainly rebuilding is  a locally driven process to protect our  communities and our investment in them  we knew that it was really going to be  critical to engage in incentivize  communities to rebuild differently in a  more rebuilt in a more resilient manner  putting back what was lost is usually  the drive after a storm there's a  there's a rush to put back exactly what  was lost we see it on the Jersey Shore  in terms of nostalgia we just want to  get there and put it back but really the  better approach is to take a moment and  pause and think about how we can come  back smarter and stronger than before in  order to do that we need to ensure that  science is the foundation of our work we  need to ensure that local decision  makers have the data that they need to  make scientifically sound decisions it  sounds like an obvious common sense for  step but it's not one that the federal  government has led with in recovery from  previous disasters  there are certainly those who ignore  science and deny climate change but when  it comes to building stronger in  communities it's the research that  matters it's the facts that matter it's  the science so the that's why our task  force partnered with the best scientists  across the federal government to empower  local governments businesses and  citizens with the information that they  need in too many communities the  standards for flood risk had not been  updated in years or even decades and  very few of them incorporate future  risks like sea level rise into how they  look at those risks as one CEO who lost  critical facilities during Hurricane  sandy said just tell me how high to  rebuild theirs there's no clear answer  though there there has not been a clear  answer from the federal government  between confusion on on FEMA maps and  just a lack of understanding about sea  level rise so we set out to answer the  question of how high do we rebuild and  ultimately issued a rebuilding strategy  to the president last month with 69  recommendations which span across the  fields of housing small business  assistance infrastructure investment  data sharing and flood insurance  affordability what makes our report  stand out to other disaster reports is  that it's not just some ideas to sit on  a shelf but rather 69 recommendations  that already have identified owners  across the federal government and  philanthropy and private enterprise one  of the things that we're most proud of  is that through the work of the task  force and Noah we developed a sea level  rise tool perhaps one that will be  useful to Yale going forward as you look  at your own tool to allow local planners  and decision-makers to click on a map  based on address and see a projection  for sea level rise as far as a century  into the future we hope that with that  knowledge and hand communities will take  the measures necessary to protect  themselves from the future floods it's a  Sandy region specific tool but the  presidents Climate Action Plan came out  and called for a similar to a nationwide  perhaps that's something we'll be able  to partner with you  on going forward another initiative of  the task force that we're particularly  excited about is the rebuild by design  design competition we are using a design  competition with philanthropic dollars  from the Rockefeller Foundation and  others to attract innovative design  solutions and ultimately build or  implement resilience projects in  communities in the sandy impacted region  we invited teams of experts to come in  and judge them based on not their design  ideas but the quality of their teams so  we have experts in engineering hydrology  Landscape Architecture many other fields  who came in and responded to our request  for qualifications we received 100 848  applications and selected 10 teams  they're working now in Connecticut New  York New Jersey around the region to try  and partner with think tanks and  universities and really figure out what  are the needs how can we comprehensively  address them with the goal that they  will ultimately tie into the billions of  dollars in federal assistance so that  when the rebuilding happens we'll be  using the best science in making the  best decisions as I close I just want to  stress that the work of the president's  Task Force isn't about looking backwards  it's about looking forward to the next  storm but it's also about a fundamental  shift in the way that communities think  about all the rebuilding that we do all  the building and all the planning should  be incorporating the risk that we face  because of climate change we have  learned through disaster recovery  analysis that for every dollar we spend  on mitigation we can see a four dollar  return when the next storm hits so  there's a lot of economic sense for  investing in mitigation on the front end  it's critical that we protect our  businesses so that we can compete and  continue operating after a storm so I  just like to say thank you again for all  of your work it's exciting to hear all  the work that's going on and I know that  working together we can ready our  communities  you
 

